  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Assert-library additions:\n\t\t\t- Has().Order generalized to arbitrary comparable keys: Order() (comparable items) and Order(it => it.Key) for any IComparable key (string, DateTime, ...); items no longer need to be comparable when a key is given\n\t\t\t- Fixes broken Order<T>() behavior when T differed from the item type (misleading failure or NullReferenceException on chaining)\n\t\t\t- Breaking (compile-time only): explicit type-argument calls like Order<Person>(p => p.Age) must drop the type argument; Order<int>() on int-collections still compiles\n\t\t\t- Dictionary assertions (on IReadOnlyDictionary): Has().Key(k), Has().Value(v), inverted with no (dict.Has().no.Key(k) reads \"has no key\"), and dict.Has(key).that for asserting the value under a key; failures list the dictionary's key-value pairs and key lookups respect the dictionary's comparer\n\t\t\t- Accessing 'that' after an inverted assertion (e.g. not.OneItem().that) now throws SetupFailed instead of yielding a meaningless default value\n\t\t\t- String assertions: Does().Match(pattern) for regex (also accepts a Regex for custom options); StringComparison overloads on Does().Contain/StartWith/EndWith (the comparison renders in the specification, e.g. \"contains \"bc\" ignoring case\"); Has().Length(n) and Has().Length().AtLeast/AtMost/InRange\n\t\t\t- Throws<T>().that: When asserting that an exception was thrown, the continuation provide the property that to make follow up assertions on the actual exception, such as the Message",
